Chapter: TransportCocite
  cites Net chapter Tcp
  cites Encode chapter DtlsHandshake

 One program citing both transports, which is the thing
 that could not be written until `DtlsAction`'s `ActSend` became
 `ActSendFlight`.

 The collision was dormant rather than harmless. `Net chapter Tcp`
 declares `ActSend (List Integer)`, carrying bytes; `Encode chapter
 DtlsHandshake` declared `ActSend (Integer)`, carrying a flight number.
 Each chapter compiled alone and each chapter's own test passed, so
 nothing in the battery could see it -- a collision needs two citers in
 one unit to exist at all. Against the pre-rename chapter this file is
 `CDX3001: Duplicate constructor: 'ActSend' is already defined`, and no
 binary is emitted.

 That is what this test is: not an assertion about DTLS or about TCP,
 but the co-citation itself. It fails by not compiling, which is why it
 carries no `.failing` sidecar -- it is the positive case, and the
 negative was run by hand against the depot revision before the rename
 landed.

 Both arms are matched and both payloads are read, so the test also
 pins that the two constructors stayed distinguishable rather than one
 shadowing the other: a list of three bytes on the TCP side, flight
 seven on the DTLS side.

Section: Arms

  cc-tcp : TcpAction -> Text
  cc-tcp (a) = when a
    is ActSend (bs) -> "tcp " & show (list-length bs)
    is otherwise -> "tcp other"

  cc-dtls : DtlsAction -> Text
  cc-dtls (a) = when a
    is ActSendFlight (f) -> "dtls " & show f
    is otherwise -> "dtls other"

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni (cc-tcp (ActSend [1, 2, 3]))
    print-line-uni (cc-dtls (ActSendFlight 7))
  end
